; =====================================================================
; cbor-seq.derived.abnf
;
; DERIVED - not verbatim. RFC 8742 ships NO grammar of any kind: no
; ABNF, no CDDL rule for the format itself, no JSON Schema. Its three
; CDDL fragments (cddl-fragments.cddl, verbatim) are about what goes
; INSIDE a byte string or INSIDE an array; two of them are array rules
; standing in for the sequence rule the language cannot write. The two
; productions below were written for this record from the prose of
; section 2 and MUST NEVER be quoted as the source's own grammar.
;
; Derived from RFC 8742:
;   section 2, the recursive definition  (rfc-8742#R-0008, #R-0009,
;              #R-0010) - the ONLY formal content in the document
;   section 2, "In short, concatenating zero or more encoded CBOR data
;              items generates a CBOR Sequence." (rfc-8742#R-0011)
;   section 2, "There is no end-of-sequence indicator." (#R-0013)
;   section 2, "CBOR Sequences, unlike JSON Text Sequences [RFC7464],
;              do not use a marker between items." (#R-0015)
;   section 1.1, "byte" is a synonym for "octet" (#R-0007)
;
; The language is ABNF (RFC 5234). Terminals are single bytes. No RFC
; 5234 core rule is used or redefined.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE WHOLE GRAMMAR
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

cbor-sequence   = *encoded-cbor-data-item

encoded-cbor-data-item = 1*OCTET
                ; NOT a grammar rule in any useful sense. See SCOPE.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; SCOPE - and this is the finding, not a disclaimer
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; The first production is EXACT and COMPLETE. Section 2's definition is
; recursive with two cases - "an empty (zero-length) sequence of bytes"
; and "the sequence of bytes making up an encoded CBOR data item
; [RFC7049] followed by a CBOR Sequence" - and `*element` is precisely
; that recursion. Nothing is lost in the translation, because there is
; nothing else in the format: no separator, no length prefix, no
; terminator, no marker, no end-of-sequence indicator.
;
; The second production is a PLACEHOLDER and is marked so. ABNF cannot
; define an encoded CBOR data item: the number of content bytes of a
; byte or text string, and the number of enclosed items of an array or
; map, are given by the argument just parsed, which is a
; context-sensitive dependency no ABNF can express. That is not this
; record's finding - it is already established in
; records/ietf/rfc-8949/distilled/schema/cbor-head.derived.abnf, which
; covers exactly the HEAD of a data item for that reason and refers the
; rest to RFC 8949 Appendix C's pseudocode (held verbatim in that
; record as well-formed.pseudocode.txt). Use those two files; do not
; try to extend this one.
;
; SO THE HONEST STATEMENT OF WHAT A GRAMMAR CAN SAY ABOUT THIS FORMAT IS
; ONE LINE LONG, AND IT IS `*item`. Three consequences follow, and all
; three are load-bearing for us:
;
;   (1) THE CONTAINER IS NOT SCHEMA-CHECKABLE, in ABNF or in CDDL.
;       Section 4.1 states the CDDL half in the document's own words.
;       This file states the ABNF half. A multi-statement file's
;       top-level rule is therefore specified in prose under every
;       candidate, which is what design-notes.md Cut 2 costs.
;
;   (2) EVERY BYTE STRING IS A PREFIX OF A VALID CBOR SEQUENCE OR
;       CONTAINS A MALFORMED ITEM, and no third category exists. `*item`
;       accepts the empty string, so a file truncated to nothing is
;       valid; it has no final-element rule, so a file truncated at an
;       item boundary is valid; and since the recursion consumes items
;       left to right, a malformed item strands everything after it
;       (#R-0021). A grammar cannot distinguish "complete" from
;       "truncated at a boundary" because the distinction is not in the
;       bytes.
;
;   (3) A LINT RULE HAS NOTHING TO TARGET. There is no production here
;       whose violation is a conformance error, which is the same thing
;       as saying section 2 carries no BCP 14 keyword (it does not: all
;       four are in sections 3, 5 and 6.3). "We use RFC 8742" cites a
;       definition, not a conformance target.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT A CONTAINER THAT *IS* EXPRESSIBLE LOOKS LIKE
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; Section 2 names the alternative itself, in a parenthesis: "(If one is
; desired, CBOR encoding an array of the CBOR data model values being
; encoded, employing either a definite or an indefinite length encoding,
; as a single CBOR data item may actually be the more appropriate
; representation.)" (#R-0014). A definite-length array head states the
; element count, is self-delimiting, and uses NO TAG - so it is exactly
; as tag-free as the sequence is, and it has a CDDL rule. The three
; candidate containers, side by side with what each one costs, are in
; cbor-seq-container.derived.cddl. The byte-level difference is ONE
; BYTE for up to 23 elements: examples/vectors.yaml D-0003 (83010203,
; 4 bytes) against D-0002 (010203, 3 bytes).
;
; [cross-check] 2026-10-21, pass 3: that citation read "V-0004 and
; V-0005", which are §4.2's two DIAGNOSTIC-NOTATION lines and carry no
; hex at all. The byte strings are D-0003 and D-0002, as
; examples/README.md's own table says ("D-0002 ... V-0005 in bytes",
; "D-0003 ... V-0004 in bytes"). Not dangling, so nothing caught it:
; bin/check-id-joins collects only RECORD-QUALIFIED ids and only the
; R/V/G/N namespaces, and both ids here were bare.
;
; Source: RFC 8742, cached source sha256
;   4462966836b60f42ebae9b5f1d2a479c328e16f0380a3434311a083bccbce30e
; =====================================================================
